right today i'd like to talk to you  about the reynolds number  the reynolds number is named after  osborne reynolds who was a british  physicist or fluid mechanics  scientist  and he was did experiments in manchester  on flow of water through pipes  and he discovered that the way in which  the water flowed depended on the size of  the pipe the diameter of the pipe and  and the speed of the water  and he characterized this these  different types of flow in terms of a  number a number that's dimensionless it  doesn't it's just a plain number it  doesn't have kilograms or meters per  second in it or anything like that it's  just a plain number  so i've got a pipe here this is the kind  of pipe that  osbourne rentals would have used and you  can see this has got a a diameter so he  was using pipes like this and passing  water through it and he also to look at  the way the water was flowing in detail  introduced ink through a tiny jet  and he discovered that at low flow rates  uh the ink jet just remained a perfect  if you inject it at the center of the  tube at this end the water's flowing  through then the inkjet remained a  perfectly nice narrow line but when the  water was flowing above a certain  velocity if she called it critical  velocity then the  flow became turbulent  so what we do is we take the velocity of  the flow  and the  diameter or  more more usually the radius of the pipe  and so we end up with an expression  looking like this so the reynolds number  not to confuse it with other symbols  like resistance we normally use re  that's just equal to  the  velocity of flow of the water in the  tube  multiplied by  the  radius of the tube and then divided by  and this is the tricky bit the viscosity  of the water all fluids have a certain  amount of viscosity water is a fluid  it's got a viscosity and the kinematic  viscosity of water is an easy number to  remember it's one divided by a million  or ten to the minus six as we say as  physicists and air has actually when you  take into account the fact that that  air's got a lower density than water uh  that has actually got a higher kinematic  viscosity  the viscosity of air is about one  divided by a hundred thousand  engineers fluid engineers need to know  about the nature of the flow of water in  pipes and around bends for that matter  all of that's very important to them  because it tells them how much water  they can get get get through the pipe  the other place it comes in of course is  is anything that moves through air when  objects like cars or aeroplanes or ships  flow through fluids they set up behind  them  vortices and when  the flow is fast enough the objects are  big enough they they set a big turb  turbulent wakes behind them and  inside those vortices and turbulent  wakes the water is all churning around  and what's happening then is that the  engine that's propelling the boat or the  airplane or whatever the energy of the  fuel of that  is is actually being converted into this  into this turbulence one of the one of  there's a beautiful illustration of  vortex pairs being formed of a nasa  photograph it's a it's a volcano  this this thing was photographed from  satellite with uh a a wind  just a westerly wind flowing over it and  you can see a beautiful  trail of vortices from behind this  volcano  and  these vortices  form it tend to form in pairs one of the  vortices is going around in an  anti-clockwise direction to you and the  other one is going around in a clockwise  direction  to you so these things are called vortex  anti-vortex pairs and they were first  studied in detail by a hungarian or  german scientist called von carmen and  these things are called one calm and  vortex streets because they like houses  on opposite side going  down the street  now that is a truly huge reynolds number  so not only are we forming  pairs of autism in this case we've got  quite a lot of turbulent air in those  vortices in and around those vortices  and we see the vortices in those in that  particular picture very beautifully  because the cloud patterns are picking  up this circular motion the clockwise  anti-clockwise motion of the airflow  inside the vortices so that's one of the  biggest reynolds numbers you can you can  see on earth  now you can get bigger reynolds numbers  than that if you look at the great red  spot of jupiter for example  the great red spot of jupiter's got a  radius comparable with the earth so much  bigger than the volcano and the wind  speeds on jupiter are much higher as  well  a few years ago we had a couple of good  students really good students doing this  project  it was called fun with flat fluids and  this is an experiment that you can do i  picked it up on the web website of  martin rutgers and he's what he's doing  his experiment is he's forming a soap  film and then as the water is flowing  vertically down the soap film what  julian is doing is putting in a tiny  obstacle something a few millimeters  across into the flow and then you can  see the video of the vortice street  forming behind the object  you
 

